WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.660
We all would prefer to only find critical&nbsp;
vulnerabilities when all you have to do is open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:13.800
a URL and a command executes. Five digits payout&nbsp;
please. But the truth is that we will keep finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:21.840
bugs with some prerequisites that downgrade&nbsp;
them to medium or even low risk. I admit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.980
I am sometimes tempted to not report a low risk&nbsp;
vulnerability but then I realize that even $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:38.580
or $200 for maybe an hour of writing a report&nbsp;
is still just too good to just throw away. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.640
today's video, I will show you a few simple rules&nbsp;
that I follow when reporting lows and mediums to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:51.720
get the maximum payout. We'll use the example&nbsp;
of my recent $2,000 report in Stripe. Enjoy!

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:03.240
The first thing that I recommend you is to work&nbsp;
with the program, not against the program. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.180
know that you report vulnerabilities to make&nbsp;
bounty and so do I. This is my main goal. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:15.300
when writing the report, literally imagine the&nbsp;
customer of the company you are reporting to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.240
How they can get harmed? And write this in&nbsp;
the report. Don't write the report about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:27.420
hypothetical user but write about the very user&nbsp;
of this company and what can happen with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.400
With that said, you should be working with&nbsp;
the team but you should not expect the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:43.140
to work with you. You should expect that&nbsp;
the triager will not make any work more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.800
than necessary to process your report. Assume&nbsp;
they won't fix your minimal errors in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:55.380
proof of concept or in your explanation.&nbsp;
Sometimes, of course, they do, but often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:01.260
they don't have too much time to process your&nbsp;
report so they will do it as quickly as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:08.760
So don't assume they will like cooperate with&nbsp;
you but assume that you have to do all the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:14.460
yourself. And sometimes, that will not be the&nbsp;
case and then you will be positively surprised.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.120
The second thing is to not treat the triagers or&nbsp;
the team as idiots and don't try to trick them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:34.140
into triaging your report with higher severity&nbsp;
than it actually deserves. Because if you set too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:41.220
high severity, they will just, first, treat you&nbsp;
as someone that's not so serious or not competent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:47.160
and they might downgrade the risk of your&nbsp;
report even lower than it actually should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.920
If your vulnerability has some limitations,&nbsp;
let's say an unlikely user interactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.720
where, for example, the user has to click&nbsp;
three times, acknowledge this in the report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.320
Imagine what would you say if you were the&nbsp;
triager and you would try to downgrade your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:14.160
own report. Think about all the arguments that you&nbsp;
could use and try to address them while writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.340
the report. Because if you don't do this, what&nbsp;
will happen is the triager will think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:26.400
anyway. At some point, the triager will reply&nbsp;
to you asking for these things and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.380
have to address them anyway. And you are in much&nbsp;
better position if you address them straight away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.340
This gives the triager the information that you&nbsp;
are aware of those limitations but you reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:45.780
the bug anyway. And probably, the triager won't&nbsp;
get back to you with your own arguments. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:53.940
the triager is more forced to ask the team if&nbsp;
they want to triage the vulnerability which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:01.620
lower risk level because of these prerequisites.&nbsp;
Of course, this won't magically help you with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:08.700
too many prerequisites. For example, if a program&nbsp;
doesn't accept XSSes without CSP bypasses, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:14.940
address this in your report, they won't magically&nbsp;
triage your bug just because of that. But also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.820
if you don't put this in your report, neither they&nbsp;
will accept the report. So you pretty much can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:27.120
lose on this. Hoping that someone will not notice&nbsp;
those prerequisites, I think it's just naive.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:37.860
I'm definitely guilty of writing a lazy report&nbsp;
when I know the bug is not worth much. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:44.340
still too much for me to just throw away if I&nbsp;
already found the bug and all I have to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:52.080
report it. This often bites back because when I&nbsp;
write a lazy report, as I called it, it comes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:58.020
to me with "Needs more info", with some questions&nbsp;
or with whatever and I end up wasting more time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.560
and much more emotions - unnecessary emotions&nbsp;
- than I would if I would just sit, spend more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.560
time initially on really good report and the bug&nbsp;
would be processed without going back and forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:16.920
That's also the reason why I am leaning&nbsp;
more towards better paying programs where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.200
of course, I will find less bugs and write&nbsp;
less reports but they are worth more because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.620
the minimum payout of, for example,&nbsp;
$500, really makes it worth the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:36.420
What do I mean by a good report? I mean a&nbsp;
report where you show the reproduction steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:42.660
from the ground - from the level zero. Because&nbsp;
you have to be aware that the triager probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:48.300
will not know the application as well as you&nbsp;
do. The company to which you report the bug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.580
of course, they will know it better than you&nbsp;
but the triager may work on multiple programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:59.880
at the same time and the triager just won't&nbsp;
know every single application in and out. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.360
try to reset your knowledge and start with the&nbsp;
very simple steps like installation of tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:12.480
setting up the environment, creating an account&nbsp;
and go from there. It's also a good practice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:20.040
simply test your vulnerability one more time&nbsp;
when creating a report because sometimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:26.220
will then find out that you made some mistake and&nbsp;
maybe you don't want to report this bug anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:34.500
It's also worth to attach any code that will help&nbsp;
the triager or the program to reproduce the issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:41.400
For example, a python proof of concept if that's&nbsp;
something more complex. If you don't know how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:48.720
write code in Python, in BBRE Premium, there is&nbsp;
a whole series of articles about using python for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.780
security - for reproducing issues, working with&nbsp;
HTTP, headless browser and so on and so forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:01.080
If there's a client-side issue that that you're&nbsp;
reporting, probably you want to create some HTML.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:08.580
Maybe just send an HTML but also, a nice tool that&nbsp;
I've used a few times is codesandbox where you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:16.740
like, host the web app on the platform. And&nbsp;
also, record demos. For simple demos where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:24.360
only a gif, I use LiceCAP because it's very quick.&nbsp;
And for more complex vulnerabilities, I sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:31.260
just use OBS - open broadcaster software and I&nbsp;
can even record a voiceover if that's necessary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.920
Hi team, I decided to record a demo because&nbsp;
there are a lot of moving parts. So first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:43.440
here on, the left hand side, I have&nbsp;
my DNS server that will return to...

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:52.860
Since I finished my challenge of 100 hours on&nbsp;
Stripe, I regularly still check new features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.440
every month when I receive the newsletter from&nbsp;
them. And a few months ago, they added something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:06.240
called Stripe Apps which is like an extension&nbsp;
of Stripe. The way it works is it works in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.480
iframe and, for example, you can quickly export&nbsp;
some files into a Google Drive or into Dropbox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:20.580
At the moment, it's still in beta version and&nbsp;
it's not yet possible to publish my own app for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:27.240
other users. I think at the moment, Stripe still&nbsp;
has to approve manually every single application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:34.440
However, it's clear from the documentation that&nbsp;
in the future, users will be able to create and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:42.360
share apps to the marketplace. My report affected&nbsp;
Apps and the attack scenario included creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.600
malicious app. And I mentioned this fact in the&nbsp;
report that, at the moment, it's not even possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:57.180
to publish my app to other users. But this subject&nbsp;
wasn't even touched upon in any message apart from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:04.200
the initial one from me. And the truth is that&nbsp;
an immature program in this moment could say:

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.199
"this bug"

00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:22.615
"not affect users"

00:09:24.476 --> 00:09:26.280
"Needs more info"

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:35.820
But here, it wasn't the case. I think most mature&nbsp;
programs like Stripe are happy that we report bugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:42.840
in alpha and beta versions because, for one, they&nbsp;
don't have to investigate if the bug was exploited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:49.560
in the wild or it wasn't because it's not&nbsp;
published for users. But it's clear it will be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:57.600
And the way it functions is that it's an iframe&nbsp;
- the app is an iframe but it's a Sandbox and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:04.320
it's well separated from the parent site. My&nbsp;
initial attack vector that I wanted to try was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:10.020
exfiltrate some unauthorized data by a malicious&nbsp;
Stripe App but I was not successful with this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:18.720
But then, I stepped back a little bit and I&nbsp;
noticed that if I put a login and password fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:25.500
in my application, LastPass or other password&nbsp;
managers can autofill my credentials into the App.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:34.440
Then, a malicious app could send these plain&nbsp;text credentials to the App's server and login&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:42.720
as the victim, giving an account takeover.&nbsp;
But the truth is that this bug is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:50.640
fault of password managers because, from the&nbsp;
browser's perspective, the iframe separation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:56.460
between Stripe normal page and&nbsp;
Stripe App was as good as it can be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.920
Then why didn't I report this bug to password&nbsp;
managers and instead to Stripe? The truth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:11.280
I did report this bug to one of the password&nbsp;
managers a few months ago and it was marked as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:18.120
duplicate of a won't fix bug from an internal&nbsp;
assessment from a few years ago. They said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:24.000
they accept the risk of that and I kinda assumed&nbsp;
that other password managers also know about this.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:35.040
And I didn't try to hide this fact in my report,&nbsp;
hoping that Stripe or the triage will not notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:41.460
this. I made it very clear in the report that I&nbsp;
think it's more of a fault of a password managers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:49.560
But Stripe is in a hard position here because&nbsp;
they can't say that it's user's responsibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:56.160
or password manager's responsibility because&nbsp;
likely, this will not protect Stripe users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:04.980
The result? No going back and forth,&nbsp;
no needs more info status and instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:13.500
$2,000 payout as a medium risk bug.&nbsp;
The way they fixed it is they removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:19.200
the autocomplete attribute and they&nbsp;
disallow password type input field so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:24.840
that there is no way that password managers&nbsp;
autofill credentials of any Stripe users.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:33.780
Stripe was actually the first program to which&nbsp;
I dedicated more time. About one year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:37.560
I challenged myself to spend 100 hours there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:43.320
If you want to see what bugs did I find and&nbsp;
how much money I made, check out this two-part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:48.480
video that's on your screen right now. For&nbsp;
now, thank you for watching and goodbye!

